# Referrer policy, named by DPIA risk R5.
#
# `no-referrer` rather than the browser default `strict-origin-when-cross-origin`,
# which still sends our origin. Kairos URLs carry member and branch UUIDs in
# their paths (/members/{id}, /departments/{id}), so a referrer sent anywhere
# off-origin discloses that a particular record was being viewed. Nothing here
# depends on referrers — there is no third-party analytics in this application,
# which the privacy notice states — so the strictest value costs nothing.
#
# Being precise about what this does and does not do, because R5 lists the
# missing header as *compounding* the image problem rather than causing it:
# this stops OUR pages leaking OUR urls. It cannot govern what imagedelivery.net
# sends when an image URL is opened directly in a tab, because that response is
# not ours to set headers on. Only authenticated delivery and deletion address
# that, and they are tracked separately on R5.

/*
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

# Apple + Android universal-link / app-link association files.
#
# Apple's spec requires application/json + no auth prompt + no redirect. The
# file has no extension, so Cloudflare Pages guesses application/octet-stream —
# force the right MIME here.

/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
  Content-Type: application/json

/.well-known/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
